Subject: Match Linux signal-number ordering in the trap fixture
Description: Peios runs Bash's upstream test suite as a build gate on
 Linux/glibc. The trap golden file lists entries in another platform's
 signal-number order, so the test fails on Linux although Bash behaves
 correctly. The fixture now expects Linux order; every trap entry and
 execution assertion is kept.
Author: Peios maintainers <packaging@peios.org>
Origin: vendor, Linux/glibc test fixture adaptation
Forwarded: no

# Linux SIGUSR1 is 10 and SIGTERM is 15; Bash prints trap entries in signal
# number order. The upstream golden file uses the opposite platform order.
# Preserve all trap content and execution assertions; change only their order.
--- a/tests/exec.right
+++ b/tests/exec.right
@@ -26,15 +26,15 @@
 0
 this is bashenv
 trap -- 'echo EXIT' EXIT
+trap -- 'echo USR1' SIGUSR1
 trap -- '' SIGTERM
-trap -- 'echo USR1' SIGUSR1
 USR1
 ./exec3.sub: line 38: /tmp/bash-notthere: No such file or directory
 ./exec3.sub: ENOENT: after failed exec: 127
 ./exec3.sub: line 43: exec: bash-notthere: not found
 trap -- 'echo EXIT' EXIT
+trap -- 'echo USR1' SIGUSR1
 trap -- '' SIGTERM
-trap -- 'echo USR1' SIGUSR1
 USR1
 EXIT
 ./execscript: line 103: notthere: command not found
